/*
 * Descriptions of the example programs for the API reference. Doxygen lists
 * each program with its source, linked to the documentation of the functions
 * it calls.
 */

/**
 * @example writing.c
 * Writes a surface as binary32 values and as 16-bit integer values, each with
 * the data type of the header set explicitly. The trailer is assembled with
 * sdfio_trailer_add_field().
 */

/**
 * @example reading.c
 * Reads a file as heights in metres of type float, prints the revision, the
 * size and the mean height, and prints the trailer.
 */

/**
 * @example streaming.c
 * Reads a file from a stream, here standard input, one row at a time with
 * sdfio_read_data() and sdfio_decode_heights(), and prints the lowest and the
 * highest height. The program holds one row in memory, not the
 * matrix.
 */

/**
 * @example counts.c
 * Writes and reads the integer counts of a scanner as 16-bit values in an
 * ISO-2.0 file and in a BCR-1.0 file. The rows are written in the order of the
 * file with sdfio_row_y(), and the points that are not measured carry the bad
 * value, which sdfio_is_bad_value() recognizes when reading.
 */

/**
 * @example time_zone.c
 * Writes and reads an ISO-1.0 file with an sdfio_time_zone. The file holds the
 * clock of the writer, and the callbacks convert the creation date between the
 * clock and UTC. The program prints the date as written, as read with the time
 * zone, and as read without it, where the clock reading of the file is the
 * date.
 */

/**
 * @example image.cpp
 * A C++ image class over the C interface: the handles belong to objects that
 * release them, a failed call is an exception that carries the message of the
 * library, and the heights are read into a two-dimensional array that is
 * indexed as `image[y][x]`.
 */

/**
 * @example opencv.cpp
 * A surface as an OpenCV depth image. A negative row stride turns the surface
 * into an image without a copy, and a region of interest is read.
 */

/**
 * @example vtk.cpp
 * Shows the SDF file given as the argument as VTK image data: the heights are
 * read in place into the array of the image, points that are not measured are
 * hidden, and the surface is displayed as a height field in a window.
 */

/**
 * @example eigen.cpp
 * A surface as an Eigen matrix: the heights are read into the matrix in place,
 * a plane is fitted by least squares for the form removal, and the matrix is
 * written back in the same layout.
 */
